A||||16||Richard Parnell||Help! Still can't get clutch to work||||9||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||05-18-2000||09:02 PM||||I've been working on the hydraulics for the clutch for two weeks now.  Last night and today I installed a new slave cylinder and clutch pedal from George at the Fiero store and also installed a new master cylinder.  The pedal feels nice and solid with no play in any direction.  Banjo is in the correct (up)position. I flushed the lines with alcohol and then DOT4 and I still can't get the car to shift gears.  After I get every thing bled I can pull the push rod back into the slave, it feels mushy.  I keep seeing some bubbles come out when I bleed the system. But I see no fluid under the car indicating a leak.  I've gone through 2 1/2 containers of DOT 4. Could the ATF have messed up something in the line somewhere?  I'm NOT using ATF now just DOT 4.  My knuckles are raw and I'm about to collapse from exhaustion.  If I can't get this to work I'm going to have to get rid of the car.  I know the clutch itself works cause if I put ATF in the system as before it will shift for a few day or weeks until the seals go bad.  What gives?||||reg||9
Z||000001||Phil||05-18-2000||09:31 PM||||One trick I've used with good sucess is to remove the slave but leave the line attached  bleed the slave with the rod pointing down. When no more bubbles come out reinstall the slave and all should be fine. Just remember that when the rod is fully extended and the system is bled everything will be in hydraulic lock and the pedal won't move so don't force it. The rod will retract as you reinstall the slave.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||05-18-2000||09:32 PM||||Could air be leaking in around the bleeder?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||05-18-2000||09:37 PM||||Phil -yes I've done that before to get all the air out.  And I know what you mean about not forcing the pedal when in lock.  Like a dummy I've bent the rod going into the master several times.  But you're saying the slave rod will push back in as you put pressure on it?  How about if I install the slave on the passenger side of the bracket that holds it, to get some more throw?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||05-18-2000||09:48 PM||||The throw length is controlled by the M/C, as long as the slave doesn't reach the end of its stroke. Just as an after thought you do have a steel pedal right? One more thing if you are in the locked situation you could put mild pressure on the pedal and see if the bleeder is leaking||||reg||1
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||05-18-2000||10:37 PM||||Phil- With the system in lock I can take the slave rod and push in the slave, it slowly goes in. Where is this pressure releasing back to?  I would have thought that it would be rigid and you would not be able to push it in.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||05-18-2000||10:45 PM||||Just curiouse, do you have any spacers placed between the slave and bracket?  I was haveing difficulty getting mine into reverse (tonight) and compared it to my buddies Gt (which is still "abandoned" in my driveway if anyone is interested) and he didnt have the spacers. <P>I removed mine, and voila... reverse.  I am also using DOT 5 brake fluid in my clutch line.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||05-18-2000||10:57 PM||||As long as your're not pushing on the pedal there is no pressure in the system the fluid flows back into the M/C tank. A small spring in the slave extends the rod to keep the clutch adjusted. I think a full throw is a little over 1" (check v8 archie-archieisms)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||05-18-2000||11:04 PM||||Richard <BR>if you're reading these posts jump onto the chat room I'll be there till about 11:15 eastern||||reg||1
Z||000009||Richard Parnell||05-18-2000||11:28 PM||||Phil- I tried getting in- it won't accept my name and went I put in a name it accepts it wants a password?||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroshop||05-19-2000||12:48 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Richard when I sent you the slave and the clutch petal I was at the Fiero Shop in Hayward, Calif. If I'm not mistaken I think that I am still there and not in Conn.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||05-19-2000||02:03 PM||||Richard,<P>My experience is that the bleeder will suck air in around the threads when the bleeder is loosened given the SLIGHTEST chance. Teflon pipe tape helps some, but air can still get in if you're not careful. <P>I have one of those 5 or 10 dollar brake bleeder things. It's a little plastic cup with a lid that separates the liquid from the air so you can use a vacuum pump with it. Unfortunately I don't have a vacuum pump. After MUCH frustration trying everything I have read and could think of, I still could not get enough air out to shift properly. About 2AM I finally decided to connect the bleeder thing to a vacuum on the plenum. I started the car, and opened the bleeder JUST enough to get the air without sucking outside air. After tightening the bleeder down, operating the clutch, and repeating this a couple of times... I now have better clutch operation than I ever thought possible!!!<P>Don't give up... It CAN be done... Good luck!!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||DRH||05-19-2000||02:06 PM||||Forgot to mention... Make sure the master cylinder cap is on tight. If the system is closed it will only pull air/fluid until the system reaches the engines vacuum level. If it can get air around the filler cap, you would probably just suck all the fluid through...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||mwbackus||05-19-2000||06:50 PM||||Using engine vacuum to bleed a hydraulic system has to be one of the most ingenious tricks I have come across to date!<P>Mike B.||||reg||7
Z||000014||87GTJohn||05-21-2000||12:21 PM||||I found that while trying to bleed the system you'll get tiny air bubble's from around the bleeder. If you place grease around the area where the bleeder screws in it helps to stop this.<BR>87GTJohn   ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Chance||06-02-2000||03:17 AM||||I just went through the SAME THING!!! I got tired of messing with it not shifting... It would sometimes after I bled the slave, but an hour later! no shift. I took it to this place, and told them "I think I need a new slave put on, after I bleed it, it works a little while, then back to the same not shifting.  I called about a week later, and they said, no, it needs a new clutch, throwout bearing, etc. all for $850.00 USD (they were the ones who installed a new clutch(hevey duty) NEW flywheel etc for 1250usd A year and a half earlier. I asked them ARE YOU SURE its that and not the slave, they said yup. so I paid the 48 bucks they charged to look at it and drove it home I told them I think a clutch should last more then a year and a half, AND I DRIVE like an old man to boot. funny thing is, when  I drove it home, it shifted perfectly fine. Im not taking it back there EVER again.||||reg||4
Z||000016||Patrick||06-02-2000||03:41 AM||||<BR><B>Chance</B>, you must be new here. Boy, do you have a lot of reading to catch up on regarding clutch problems... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
